> You’re thinking of **scenario reduction** / **scenario selection** methods—specifically things like:
> 
> - **Forward selection scenario reduction**  
> - **Backward (or fast forward) scenario reduction**  
> - More generally: **scenario reduction algorithms** in stochastic programming
> 
> These are *heuristic* or *approximate* methods that take a (possibly huge) set of \(K\) points in \(\mathbb{R}^D\) (scenarios) and identify a smaller subset that:
> 
> 1. Lies roughly on or near the “outer envelope” of the data (approximate convex hull / support), and  
> 2. Preserves some probability structure (e.g., minimizing Wasserstein distance between the original and reduced empirical distributions).
> 
> They’re often described as “heuristic identification of representative scenarios” or “scenario identification” and are widely used in multistage stochastic optimization, energy planning, finance, etc.
> 
> If you were instead thinking of a purely geometric method (no probabilities):
> 
> - **ε–coresets for convex hulls**  
> - **Approximate convex hull via greedy extreme point selection** (a.k.a. farthest-point or extremal-point heuristics)
> 
> Those are usually called “approximate convex hull algorithms” or “extreme point selection heuristics,” not “scenario identification,” though people in optimization sometimes blur that language.
