> That's not right. It's name refers to the fact that it can pick out kind of characteristic elements, and then all actual points are a weighted sum of those. I remember learning about it about the same time as PCA.
**assistant**:
> You’re describing **archetypal analysis**.
> 
> - It finds a small set of **archetypes** (characteristic points) in your \(K\)-by-\(D\) data.
> - Each archetype is itself a **convex combination** of the original data points.
> - Each original data point is then approximated as a **convex combination of the archetypes** (i.e., a weighted sum with nonnegative weights that sum to 1).
> - Geometrically, the archetypes sit on or near the **convex hull** of the data, and the data lie inside the convex hull of the archetypes.
> 
> It’s often presented around the same time as PCA but is more “extreme-point / scenario-like” in flavor, which matches what you remembered.
